The \pkg{posterior} package is intended to provide useful tools
for both users and developers of packages for fitting Bayesian models or
working with output from Bayesian models. The primary goals of the package
are to:
\itemize{
\item Efficiently convert between many different useful formats of
draws (samples) from posterior or prior distributions.
\item Provide consistent methods for operations commonly performed on draws,
for example, subsetting, binding, or mutating draws.
\item Provide various summaries of draws in convenient formats.
\item Provide lightweight implementations of state of the art posterior inference
diagnostics.
}
}
\section{Package options}{


The following options are used to format and print \code{\link{draws}} objects,
as in \code{print.draws_array()}, \code{print.draws_df()}, \code{print.draws_list()},
\code{print.draws_matrix()}, and \code{print.draws_rvars()}:
\itemize{
\item \code{posterior.max_draws}: Maximum number of draws to print.
\item \code{posterior.max_iterations}: Maximum number of iterations to print.
\item \code{posterior.max_chains}: Maximum number of chains to print.
\item \code{posterior.max_variables}: Maximum number of variables to print.
}

The following options are used for formatting the output of
\code{\link{summarize_draws}}:
\itemize{
\item \code{posterior.num_args}: Arguments passed to \link[tibble:num]{num()}
for pretty printing of summaries.
}

The following options are used to format and print \code{\link{rvar}} objects,
as in \code{print.rvar()} and \code{print.draws_rvars()}:
\itemize{
\item \code{posterior.rvar_summary}: What style of summary to display:
\code{"mean_sd"} displays \verb{mean ± sd}, \code{"median_mad"} displays \verb{median ± mad}.
\item \code{posterior.digits}: How many significant digits are displayed. This
defaults to a smaller value (\code{2}) than \code{getOption("digits")} because
\code{\link{rvar}}s print two numbers (point summary and uncertainty) next to
each other.
}

The following option is used to construct new \code{\link{rvar}} objects,
as in \code{rfun()} and \code{rdo()}:
\itemize{
\item \code{posterior.rvar_ndraws}: The number of draws used to construct
new random variables when this number cannot be determined
from existing arguments (e.g., other \code{\link{rvar}}s passed to a function).
}

The following options are used to control warning messages:
\itemize{
\item \code{posterior.warn_on_merge_chains}: (logical) Some operations will
trigger an automatic merging of chains, for example, because chains do not
match between two objects involved in a binary operation. Whether this
causes a warning can be controlled by this option.
}
}

\section{Warning and error classes}{


Every warning signaled by \pkg{posterior} has the class
\code{posterior_warning} and one class for its cause. Use these classes to
handle or muffle a specific warning, for example with
\code{\link[=withCallingHandlers]{withCallingHandlers()}} and \code{\link[=invokeRestart]{invokeRestart()}}\code{("muffleWarning")}.
\itemize{
\item \code{posterior_warning_ess_capped}: An ESS estimate was capped.
\item \code{posterior_warning_superchain_ids_length}: The length of
\code{superchain_ids} does not equal the number of chains.
\item \code{posterior_warning_superchain_unequal}: The superchains do not have the
same number of chains.
\item \code{posterior_warning_non_numeric}: A conversion dropped non-numeric
variables.
\item \code{posterior_warning_chains_dropped}: Chains were dropped.
\item \code{posterior_warning_no_variables}: No variables with unreserved names
were found.
\item \code{posterior_warning_odd_iterations}: The number of iterations is not
even.
\item \code{posterior_warning_pit_above_one}: Some PIT values are larger than 1.
\item \code{posterior_warning_zero_weight}: All draws of a variable have zero
weight.
\item \code{posterior_warning_nans_produced}: NaNs were produced.
\item \code{posterior_warning_draws_df_dropped}: The \code{draws_df} class was dropped.
\item \code{posterior_warning_pareto_invalid_input}: The input to Pareto smoothing
contains infinite or \code{NA} values.
\item \code{posterior_warning_pareto_tail_too_short}: The number of tail draws was
increased to 5.
\item \code{posterior_warning_pareto_tail_exceeds_draws}: The number of tail draws
is not less than the total number of draws.
\item \code{posterior_warning_pareto_tail_too_long}: The number of tail draws is
more than half of the total number of draws.
\item \code{posterior_warning_pareto_tail_not_fitted}: The generalized Pareto
distribution was not fitted because there are fewer than 5 tail draws.
}

The error signaled when all weights are zero has the class
\code{posterior_error_degenerate_weights}.
